  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The Department of Homeland Security has $53B in contract obligations for FY2026 to date. That total comes from 20,967 awards with an average award size of $2.5M, and obligations are up 87.1% year over year. Fiscal-year obligations rose steadily from $19.7B in FY21 to $23.9B in FY23, held near $23.6B in FY24, increased to $28.3B in FY25, and reached $53B in FY26. The spending mix for FY2026 to date shows a heavy concentration in construction and ship work, with Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $28.4B and Ship Building and Repairing at $4.3B. Security Guards and Patrol Services total $3.2B, and services such as Process, Physical Distribution, and Logistics Consulting Services and Computer Systems Design Services total $2.6B and $1.7B respectively. The top vendors reflect that mix: FISHER SAND & GRAVEL CO accounts for $13.4B and BARNARD CONSTRUCTION COMPANY, INCORPORATED accounts for $4.5B. The FY2026 to date data show large-dollar construction and maritime programs alongside recurring services and IT work.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Why did DHS obligations rise in FY2026 to date?",
        "body": "Obligations rose to $53B in FY2026 to date, up 87.1% year over year. Fiscal-year obligations climbed from $28.3B in FY25 to $53B in FY26. A large share of FY2026 to date obligations is in Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $28.4B and Ship Building and Repairing at $4.3B. Major vendor awards, including $13.4B to FISHER SAND & GRAVEL CO, also contribute to the increase."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Where is DHS spending concentrated in FY2026 to date?",
        "body": "Spending is concentrated in construction and maritime sectors, led by Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $28.4B and Ship Building and Repairing at $4.3B. Security and logistics services are sizable, with Security Guards and Patrol Services at $3.2B and Process, Physical Distribution, and Logistics Consulting Services at $2.6B. IT and professional services include Computer Systems Design Services at $1.7B and Other Computer Related Services at $1.4B. Engineering and precision manufacturing categories appear at $823M and $818M respectively."
      }
    ]
  },
  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How much does Department of Homeland Security spend on contracts?",
      "a": "DHS spends $53B on contracts in FY2026 to date. That total comes from 20,967 awards with an average award size of $2.5M, and obligations are up 87.1% year over year. Fiscal-year obligations by year were $19.7B in FY21, $20.8B in FY22, $23.9B in FY23, $23.6B in FY24, $28.3B in FY25, and $53B in FY26."
    },
    {
      "q": "What does Homeland Security buy?",
      "a": "Homeland Security buys a large amount of construction and ship work, led by Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $28.4B and Ship Building and Repairing at $4.3B. The department also buys Security Guards and Patrol Services at $3.2B, Process, Physical Distribution, and Logistics Consulting Services at $2.6B, Computer Systems Design Services at $1.7B, and Other Computer Related Services at $1.4B. Additional categories include Engineering Services at $823M and Search, Detection, Navigation, Guidance, Aeronautical, and Nautical System and Instrument Manufacturing at $818M."
    },
    {
      "q": "Who are Homeland Security's largest contractors?",
      "a": "Homeland Security's largest contractors in FY2026 to date are FISHER SAND & GRAVEL CO at $13.4B and BARNARD CONSTRUCTION COMPANY, INCORPORATED at $4.5B. Other top vendors are SLS FEDERAL SERVICES LLC at $2.4B, SPENCER CONSTRUCTION LLC at $2.4B, SOUTHWEST VALLEY CONSTRUCTORS CO at $2.2B, AMI METALS, INC at $1.5B, BOLLINGER SHIPYARDS LOCKPORT, L.L.C. at $1.3B, RAUMA MARINE CONSTRUCTIONS OY at $1.1B, DAVIE DEFENSE INC. at $957M, and CSI AVIATION, INC at $833M."
